# CHMOD(1)

## NAME

chmod - change mode

## SYNOPSIS

| **chmod** mode file ...

## DESCRIPTION

The mode of each named file is changed according to *mode,* which may be absolute or symbolic.
An absolute *mode* is an octal number constructed from the OR of the following modes:

4000
: | set user ID on execution

2000
: | set group ID on execution

1000
: | sticky bit, see chmod(2)

0400
: | read by owner

0200
: | write by owner

0100
: | execute (search in directory) by owner

0070
: | read, write, execute (search) by group

0007
: read, write, execute (search) by others

A symbolic *mode* has the form:

> [*who*] *op permission* [*op permission*] ...

The *who* part is a combination of the letters **u** (for user's permissions), **g** (group)
and **o** (other). The letter **a** stands for **ugo.** If *who* is omitted, the default is *a*
but the setting of the file creation mask (see umask(2)) is taken into account.

*Op* can be **+** to add *permission* to the file's mode, **-** to take away *permission* and
**=** to assign *permission* absolutely (all other bits will be reset).

*Permission* is any combination of the letters **r** (read), **w** (write), **x** (execute),
**s** (set owner or group id) and **t** (save text - sticky). Letters **u, g** or **o**
indicate that *permission* is to be taken from the current mode. Omitting *permission* is only
useful with **=** to take away all permissions.

The first example denies write permission to others, the second makes a file executable:

> | chmod o-w file
> | chmod +x file

Multiple symbolic modes separated by commas may be given. Operations are performed in the order
specified. The letter **s** is only useful with **u** or **g.**

Only the owner of a file (or the super-user) may change its mode. That rule is chmod(2)'s and
not this program's: the call goes through **owner()** in kernel/fio.c, which admits the file's
owner as well as root. *Chmod* is therefore **not** a set-user-ID program, unlike mkdir(1),
mv(1) and rmdir(1) on this system.

**Note: chmod +s** does nothing at all, here and in v7 alike, and the sentence above
understates it. With *who* omitted the default is **a** less the file creation mask, which is
**01777; s** is **06000;** and the two have no bit in common. The letter **s** is not merely
`only useful` with **u** or **g**, it is inert without them.

**Note: chmod +t** does work - the sticky bit **01000** is inside **01777** - but only for the
super-user. Chmod(2) here reads

> **if (u.u_uid) uap->fmode &= ~ISVTX;**

\- kernel/sys4.c - so an ordinary owner's **chmod +t** succeeds and silently changes nothing.

**Note:** the omitted-*who* default depends on umask(2), and on this system the initial mask
(**CMASK** in <sys/param.h>) is **0**, while no shell here has a **umask** builtin. So the
default is plainly **a** today, and every mode this port's tests predict rests on that.

**Note:** the mode argument is parsed **once, before any file is opened,** so an unparsable
mode changes nothing rather than half of what was named. An absolute mode out of range is not
diagnosed: *chmod* accumulates it and chmod(2) masks it with **07777**.

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Chmod* exits with the number of files it could not change, or **255** for a usage error or an
unparsable mode. All four messages go to the standard error, unlike chown(1)'s and ln(1)'s.

**Usage: chmod [ugoa][+-=][rwxstugo] file ...**

**chmod: invalid mode**

**chmod: can't access** *name* - the file could not be *stat*\&ed.

**chmod: can't change** *name* - the file was found and chmod(2) refused it.

## SEE ALSO

ls(1), chmod(2), chown (1), stat(2), touch(1), umask(2)
